```js
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';

const text = readFileSync(0, 'utf8');
const chars = [...text.replace(/\n/g, '')].length;
const words = text.split(/[ \t\n]/).filter(Boolean).length;
const lines = text.split('\n').length;
console.log(`${chars} ${words} ${lines}`);
```

readFileSync(0, 'utf8') reads stdin. On Windows this works in Node? readFileSync(0) on Windows with stdin — historically there were issues with readFileSync(0) on Windows (it can throw EAGAIN or work depending). Safer: read stdin via async stream:

```js
let text = '';
process.stdin.setEncoding('utf8');
for await (const chunk of process.stdin) text += chunk;
```

Top-level await works in .mjs. That's cross-platform safe.

Word splitting: whitespace chars are space, \t, \n per spec. Use split(/[ \t\n]+/).filter(s => s.length > 0). Note filter(Boolean) would also drop empty strings; but careful: could there be other whitespace like \r? Spec says only space, \t, \n. If input has \r, it's not a separator, so "\r" would count as a word segment. Follow spec exactly: split on space, \t, \n only.

Chars: remove \n only, count codepoints via [...].

Lines: text.split('\n').length.

